WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.941
- [Narrator] World War
II saw lots of heroes,

00:00:03.941 --> 00:00:07.499
Winston Churchill, Charles de
Gaulle, Commander Eisenhower,

00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:10.789
but one you probably
don't recognize is Smokey,

00:00:10.789 --> 00:00:15.181
a Yorkshire Terrier, and the
tiniest war hero of all time.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.681
(dog panting)

00:00:20.021 --> 00:00:21.280
- [Man Voiceover] I feel Smokey and I

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.989
were destined to be together.

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.320
- [Narrator] That's Bill
Wynne, a corporal at the time

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.159
and Smokey's owner.

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:31.691
He bought Smokey for $6.44
during his time serving

00:00:31.691 --> 00:00:34.061
in the Pacific theater.

00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:35.461
- [Bill Voiceover] I
think that if she’d fallen

00:00:35.461 --> 00:00:37.189
into anyone else's hands,

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:39.629
she would never have been what she was.

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:41.461
- [Narrator] And she was remarkable.

00:00:41.461 --> 00:00:42.294
(dog panting)

00:00:42.294 --> 00:00:43.260
(toy squeaking)

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.261
Smokey followed Bill
through 150 air raids,

00:00:47.261 --> 00:00:49.939
12 combat missions, and once even led Bill

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:52.429
to safety during a Japanese bombing.

00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:54.533
- [Bill Voiceover] Kaboom, kaboom, kaboom.

00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:56.012
Well we'd look around,

00:00:56.012 --> 00:00:58.875
we got eight of our buddies all wounded

00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:00.811
around where Smokey and I were.

00:01:00.811 --> 00:01:02.463
If Smokey hadn't taken me there,

00:01:02.463 --> 00:01:05.232
I would've been standing
with the other guys.

00:01:05.232 --> 00:01:06.065
- [Voiceover] Good dog.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.083
- [Narrator] Smokey also
accompanied nurses on their rounds

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:10.395
at a US Army hospital in New Guinea.

00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:12.035
- [Bill Voiceover] Smokey
gets credit for being

00:01:12.035 --> 00:01:14.475
the first therapy dog on record.

00:01:14.475 --> 00:01:16.053
(dog panting)

00:01:16.053 --> 00:01:18.154
- [Narrator] Back in Bill's
hometown of Cleveland,

00:01:18.154 --> 00:01:20.819
Smokey became famous
doing tricks on TV shows.

00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:21.773
(dog barking)

00:01:21.773 --> 00:01:23.571
- [Bill Voiceover] She
could walk on a drum,

00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:25.064
she could ride a scooter.

00:01:25.064 --> 00:01:27.851
- [Narrator] Smokey died
a celebrity in 1957,

00:01:27.851 --> 00:01:29.803
but she wasn't forgotten.

00:01:29.803 --> 00:01:31.075
She has memorials in--

00:01:31.075 --> 00:01:33.664
- [Bill Voiceover] Ohio,
Pennsylvania, Australia,

00:01:33.664 --> 00:01:35.653
Hawaii, Tennessee.

00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:37.864
- [Narrator] But her biggest legacy...

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.464
Popular belief is that Smokey's the reason

00:01:39.464 --> 00:01:42.653
that Yorkies are such a popular pet.

00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:44.853
So thank Smokey the World War II dog

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:46.803
for all those cute little Yorkies.

00:01:46.803 --> 00:01:47.841
(dog barking)

00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:50.841
(dog toy squeaking)

